Yes. The new cache key is too narrow, and that makes the cache unsound.

**What the new key drops.** The old call passed the range proof, value commitment, asset commitment, and scriptPubKey. The new `ComputeEntry` hashes only the nonce, the proof, and the value commitment. But the range proof verification that follows the cache check takes all four. In Elements, the proof is verified against the Pedersen commitment, with the asset commitment parsed as the generator and the scriptPubKey passed as the extra commitment data. A proof that is valid for one generator or one script is not valid for another.

**Why that matters.** A cache entry populated by a valid verification of (proof, value commitment, asset A, script S) will now return true for the same proof and value commitment paired with a different asset commitment or a different scriptPubKey, without ever calling the verifier. The binding of the proof to the output script is exactly the thing the extra commitment exists to enforce. Even if an attacker cannot turn this into inflation directly, it is a consensus divergence: a node with a warm cache accepts an output that a cold-cache node rejects. That is the same bug class that signature caches have to avoid by keying on the scriptCode.

**One thing the diff gets right, which may be why it was written.** The old code was also broken, just differently. It wrapped the value commitment in a `CPubKey`. A commitment starts with 0x08 or 0x09, which `CPubKey::GetLen` does not recognize, so `Set` fails and the key is invalidated to size zero. The old hash therefore wrote zero bytes for the value commitment and never bound the cache entry to it. The new code fixes that but swaps one omission for two.

**The fix.** Keep the new function but hash all of the verifier's inputs:

```cpp
void ComputeEntry(uint256& entry,
                  const std::vector<unsigned char>& proof,
                  const std::vector<unsigned char>& valueCommitment,
                  const std::vector<unsigned char>& assetCommitment,
                  const CScript& scriptPubKey)
{
    CSHA256()
        .Write(nonce.begin(), nonce.size())
        .Write(proof.data(), proof.size())
        .Write(valueCommitment.data(), valueCommitment.size())
        .Write(assetCommitment.data(), assetCommitment.size())
        .Write(scriptPubKey.data(), scriptPubKey.size())
        .Finalize(entry.begin());
}
```

Two smaller notes. Length-prefixing the variable-size fields, or at least the proof, would remove any ambiguity about where one field ends and the next begins. And if the two cache instances ever share storage, the preimage layout here differs from the signature cache's, so the nonce is the only thing keeping the domains separate. Both are minor next to the missing fields.
